WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.969 --> 00:00:19.592
I started being interested in math originally
in connection to the Math Olympiads.

00:00:19.592 --> 00:00:23.480
In Paris they instituted The Instituto Nacional
de Matemática Pura e Aplicada, Institute

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:25.280
for Pure and Applied Math here.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:26.940
It's mostly a research institute.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:32.629
It's very receptive for young people, so I
started here already straight from the Olympiads,

00:00:32.629 --> 00:00:33.860
so still in high school.

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:42.100
At IMPA, dynamical systems is a very big part,
and one researcher here started talking to me.

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:47.000
I started liking the problems, and I started
doing dynamical systems after.

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:51.699
One example of a dynamical system includes
this kind of billiards that are very simplified

00:00:51.699 --> 00:00:52.780
mathematical models.

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:57.100
You have a ball that moves and reflects elastically
off the boundaries.

00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:02.010
They can consider something like a regular
pentagon, instead of rectangular billiards.

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:05.320
And you ask: What are the properties of these
dynamical systems?

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:08.750
So: What happens to the long-term behavior
of the particle?

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:12.390
You could ask what's the difference between
the different polygons.

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:15.040
From the point of view of dynamical systems,
you are looking at the whole global parameter

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:16.040
space.

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:20.250
One day I realized that some of the things
that I had computed at the technical level

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:25.080
for this kind of more usual analysis that
was available, what I tend to call one-frequence

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:30.141
shredding operators, was possibly unlocking
the possibilities to having a view of the

00:01:30.141 --> 00:01:31.141
whole parameter space.

00:01:31.141 --> 00:01:35.560
I knew that there were things that needed
to be done on the technical side, but now

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:38.510
it was within the realm of the possible.

00:01:38.510 --> 00:01:43.760
Right now, I spend part of my time in Paris,
part of my time here in Rio.

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:48.060
I like to work in a nice setting — walking
along the beach.

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:52.760
You just hear the waves, and you can just
forget about all of the noise and so on.

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:56.330
I was surprised when I heard that I got the
prize.

00:01:56.330 --> 00:02:00.960
The president of the IMU sent me an email
asking for a phone number, that she needed

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:01.960
to contact me.

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:05.540
In this moment I understood that it is likely
about the prize.

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:08.530
I felt very happy.

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:11.230
Most of all, more relaxed at that moment.

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:14.900
Of course it's good for both Brazil and France.

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:17.220
Brazil never had Fields medal or Nobel Prize.

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:21.360
I think it's very motivational for many people
here.

